// .devcontainer/devcontainer.json — [#554] NB4-G stage 1: the off-machine lane substrate.
//
// WHY THIS EXISTS: a lane that runs off this workstation must run on a *provisioned* host.
// The measured failure shape is not "the container won't build" — it is a container that
// builds fine and then runs gates that are silently vacuous: the wrong `uv`, a shallow clone
// (every history-dependent detector reads as clean), or unarmed git hooks. Intake #39 §D and
// [#554]'s four legs name those traps; `provision.sh` ASSERTS each one closed and REFUSES
// rather than logging.
//
// SINGLE SOURCE OF PINS: this file declares no version it does not have to. `devcontainer.json`
// is static JSON and cannot read a file, so every pin that has a home in the repo is read at
// provision time by `provision.sh` from that home, never restated here:
//   * uv version   <- pyproject.toml [tool.uv] required-version   (ADR-106: `==0.11.19`)
//   * interpreter  <- .python-version                             (`3.12.10`, installed BY uv)
//   * deps         <- uv.lock via `uv sync --locked`
// The base-image tag below is the ONE version token this file carries, and it is a floor, not
// the pin: uv installs the exact `.python-version` interpreter for the project venv and
// `provision.sh` asserts the result, so the image's own Python never decides what the gates run
// on. Bumping `.python-version` therefore needs no edit here.
//
// PORTABILITY (row Done-when clause 2): nothing below is Codespaces-specific. The identical
// pair of files runs on a VPS via `devcontainer up --workspace-folder .` — that is the whole
// point of using the devcontainer spec rather than a Codespaces-only setup script.
{
  "name": "dev-knowledge — lane substrate (NB4-G stage 1)",

  // Debian-based devcontainer image with git + a system Python floor. The `1-3.12-bookworm`
  // tag tracks the 3.12 line, matching pyproject's `requires-python = ">=3.12"`; the EXACT
  // interpreter is provisioned by uv from `.python-version`. The tag itself is unchanged and
  // still the ONE version token this file carries — it moved into `Dockerfile`'s `BASE_IMAGE`
  // arg, which is built here instead of used directly because that image's apt is broken
  // upstream (unsigned yarn repo). `Dockerfile` carries the full measurement; without it no SSH
  // server can be installed in the container and no feature can build.
  "build": {
    "dockerfile": "Dockerfile"
  },

  // THE REACHABILITY LEG — witnessed, not anticipated. The image above carries no
  // openssh-server, and Codespaces injects one only when the devcontainer asks for it. Without
  // this feature `gh codespace ssh` AND `gh codespace logs` both refuse with "failed to start
  // SSH server", which means a codespace built from this file comes up perfectly and is then
  // reachable ONLY from a browser — no CLI, no agent, no way to read the provisioning log that
  // proves the four legs closed. An off-machine LANE substrate that no lane can be driven on is
  // not a substrate, so this belongs with the four legs rather than in the customizations block.
  // Costs nothing off-Codespaces: `devcontainer up` on a VPS installs the same sshd and the
  // identical-script clause is preserved.
  // THE RUNTIME LEG — the container answered this one in its own words. A codespace built from
  // the file as it stood came up Available, reachable, fully provisioned, and then refused the
  // only thing it exists to do: the `Dispatch-Codespace` runner guard exited 91 with
  //   {"error":"claude is not installed in this devcontainer"}
  // A substrate that provisions perfectly and carries no agent is not a lane substrate, so the
  // runtime belongs beside sshd in the four-legs half of this file rather than in
  // `customizations` — the same reasoning the reachability leg above already carries.
  //
  // WHY THE FEATURE AND NOT A `RUN` LINE. Derived from the current official doc, not chosen:
  // code.claude.com/docs/en/devcontainer §"Add Claude Code to your dev container" states
  // "Claude Code installs into any dev container through the Claude Code Dev Container Feature"
  // and prints exactly the `features` entry below. The Dockerfile `RUN`/npm path IS documented,
  // but only as the alternative for pinning the CLI ("To pin a specific Claude Code version for
  // reproducible builds, install it from your Dockerfile with
  // `npm install -g @anthropic-ai/claude-code@X.Y.Z` instead of using the feature, and set
  // `DISABLE_AUTOUPDATER`"). The doc's devcontainer recommendation is the feature; this file
  // takes the recommended path.
  //
  // WHAT THE `:1.0` TAG ACTUALLY PINS, stated because it is easy to over-read. The doc: "The
  // version tag at the end, such as `:1.0`, pins the feature's install script, not the Claude
  // Code release. The feature installs the latest Claude Code, and Claude Code auto-updates
  // itself inside the container by default." So this pins the INSTALLER (live feature version
  // 1.0.5 at the time of writing) and deliberately floats the CLI. That is the pinnable unit the
  // doc offers on this path, and floating the CLI is right here for the same reason the base
  // image is a floor and not a pin: a disposable container gets the agent the operator's
  // subscription is entitled to, and no version token enters this file that does not have to.
  // Pinning the CLI is a one-way door away from the feature — it costs the Dockerfile npm path
  // plus `DISABLE_AUTOUPDATER` — and is taken only if a measured run needs reproducibility.
  //
  // NODE: NOT DECLARED, and the omission is derived rather than lucky. The doc says "The Claude
  // Code feature installs Node.js itself when the base image doesn't provide it", and names the
  // node feature only as the repair for a specific measured failure ("If that install fails and
  // the build stops with `Failed to install Node.js and npm`, add
  // `"ghcr.io/devcontainers/features/node:1": {}`"). This base image DOES provide it — the
  // evidence is in `Dockerfile`'s own measurement, which exists solely because the image ships a
  // yarn apt source, and yarn arrives with that image's Node. If a build ever prints that exact
  // string, the doc's repair is the fix; declaring it pre-emptively would be inventing a
  // dependency the doc conditions on a failure that has not happened.
  //
  // PORTABILITY is preserved, as with sshd: `devcontainer up` on a VPS installs the same feature.
  // F1 (2026-08-31): `ghcr.io/anthropics/devcontainer-features/claude-code:1.0` was REMOVED.
  // Measured, twice, including on a `gh codespace rebuild --full` that busts the image cache:
  // the feature leaves NO `claude` binary anywhere on the filesystem while `creation.log`
  // records `Outcome: success`. A toolless container that reads as a successful build is the
  // worst available failure shape -- a lane dispatched into it returns Ok=True in under a
  // minute with an empty result. The install moved to `provision.sh`, which uses the install
  // path Anthropic's own docs call recommended (`curl -fsSL https://claude.ai/install.sh`,
  // native binary, no Node runtime dependency) and ASSERTS the binary afterwards, so the
  // silent-success shape is structurally unavailable.
  "features": {
    "ghcr.io/devcontainers/features/sshd:1": {
      "version": "latest"
    }
  },

  // THE ENV GATE ([#554] leg 4) IS NOT DECLARED HERE, AND THE ABSENCE IS THE FIX.
  //
  // This file previously carried
  //   "containerEnv": { "DEV_KNOWLEDGE_PROVISION_STAMP": "${containerEnv:HOME}/..." }
  // on the reasoning that a VPS host might want to relocate the stamp. Measured on
  // 2026-08-19 (`docs/audits/2026-08-19-technical-554-proof.md` §2.1): `containerEnv` cannot
  // reference `containerEnv` — a variable cannot reference the block being defined — so the
  // literal string `${containerEnv:HOME}/.dev-knowledge-provision-stamp` reached
  // `provision.sh`, whose `mkdir -p "$(dirname ...)"` then created a directory *named*
  // `${containerEnv:HOME}` inside the working tree on every single container start.
  //
  // The relocation capability is NOT lost: `provision.sh` still honours an exported
  // `DEV_KNOWLEDGE_PROVISION_STAMP` and falls back to `${HOME}/.dev-knowledge-provision-stamp`,
  // which is what the comment above always claimed the value was. A VPS host that genuinely
  // wants it elsewhere exports the variable; a host that does not says nothing and gets a
  // stamp in `$HOME` rather than a junk directory in the repo. The script additionally
  // REFUSES a stamp path that still carries an unexpanded `${`, so this whole class fails
  // loudly at the next host that tries it instead of being rediscovered by measurement.

  // THE CREDENTIAL LEG — and the first thing to understand is that this block does NOT perform
  // the exposure. Stating that plainly, because a `secrets` key reads like a transport and is
  // not one.
  //
  // HOW THE TOKEN ACTUALLY REACHES THE CONTAINER: by itself, from GitHub, with no repo-side
  // wiring at all. docs.github.com, "Managing your account-specific secrets for GitHub
  // Codespaces": "A development environment secret is exported as an environment variable into
  // the user's terminal session." Scope and timing come from the same page — "You can choose
  // which repositories should have access to each secret. Then, you can use the secret in any
  // codespace you create for a repository that has access to the secret." and "Once you have
  // created a secret, it will be available when you create a new codespace or restart the
  // codespace." Anthropic's devcontainer page says the same thing from the other side: "To carry
  // authentication across codespaces, store `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` or a `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN`
  // from `claude setup-token` as a Codespaces secret. Codespaces exposes secrets as environment
  // variables inside the container automatically."
  //
  // The operator has already created `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` as a user-level secret scoped to
  // this repository. So the transport is closed, and it was closed by an act outside this file.
  //
  // WHAT THIS BLOCK IS THEN, AND WHY IT EARNS ITS PLACE: it is the repo-side DECLARATION, the
  // only mechanism GitHub documents for a repository to state a user-provided secret it needs.
  // docs.github.com, "Specifying recommended secrets for a repository": "If a project needs
  // user-specific secrets to be set up, you can prompt people to do this when they create a
  // codespace." — "Edit the `devcontainer.json` file, adding the `secrets` property at the top
  // level of the file, within the enclosing JSON object." — "After you specify recommended
  // secrets, if people have not already created those secrets in their personal settings for
  // Codespaces, they will be prompted to do so when they use the advanced options method of
  // creating a codespace." The doc's own rule for when to use it names this exact case: "You
  // should use recommended secrets for development environment secrets that the user who creates
  // the codespace, rather than the owner of the repository or organization, must provide."
  //
  // HONEST LIMIT, in the shape `provisioning.yaml` already uses: this DECLARES, it does not
  // enforce. It cannot create the secret, cannot verify one exists, and a codespace created
  // without it comes up fine and fails later at the agent rather than here. It converts an
  // invisible prerequisite into a stated one and prompts a creator who has not set it — that is
  // the whole of what the mechanism does, and claiming more of it would be the vacuous-gate
  // failure this substrate's provisioning was built to refuse.
  //
  // WHY NOT `containerEnv` / `remoteEnv`: the exposure is already automatic, so a passthrough
  // would add a second declaration of the same variable with nothing to gain — and `containerEnv`
  // specifically is the block [#554] removed above, which stays removed.
  //
  // NO `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`, ANYWHERE, DELIBERATELY. The credential is the SUBSCRIPTION OAuth
  // token from `claude setup-token`: model-requests only, usage counted against the operator's
  // plan, no separate API invoice. Two sources, kept distinct rather than blurred into one
  // claim: Anthropic's setup page documents that the key PREEMPTS the account sign-in path —
  // "If the `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` environment variable is set, Claude Code prompts you once to
  // approve the key instead of opening a browser" — and the BILLING consequence of that
  // precedence, that a present key silently moves lane spend off-subscription, is the operator's
  // standing ruling, not something the doc asserts. Naming only the OAuth token here is the
  // enforcement actually available at this layer.
  //
  // PORTABILITY: `secrets` is Codespaces-specific and inert elsewhere; `devcontainer up` on a
  // VPS ignores it, and that host supplies the same variable by its own means. The
  // identical-script clause is untouched.
  //
  // Exposure and rotation are recorded in the standing register `protocols/STANDING_RULINGS.md`
  // (landed by a sibling lane): the token grants the operator's subscription model access to any
  // lane running in this container, rotation is a re-run of `claude setup-token`, expiry ~1 year.
  "secrets": {
    "CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN": {
      "description": "Subscription OAuth token from `claude setup-token` — the credential a lane's headless run authenticates with inside this container. Set it as a USER-level Codespaces secret scoped to this repository. Do NOT set ANTHROPIC_API_KEY: it takes precedence and would move lane spend off-subscription.",
      "documentationUrl": "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/authentication#generate-a-long-lived-token"
    }
  },

  // THE PREBUILD LEG (lane contract A3), in the only form a repo file can carry it.
  //
  // A Codespaces prebuild executes `onCreateCommand` and `updateContentCommand` and bakes their
  // result into the prebuilt image; `postCreateCommand` is NEVER prebuilt — it runs at codespace
  // creation, every time. So with provisioning wired only to `postCreateCommand`, enabling a
  // prebuild would cache the ~85 s container build and leave the whole provisioning run on the
  // create path. Naming the same script here moves it inside the cacheable half.
  //
  // Running it twice on a NON-prebuilt create is deliberate and costs almost nothing: C1 makes
  // the script idempotent, and a fully-provisioned re-run measured **7 s** in a live container
  // on 2026-08-21 (against ~28 s for the cold run it follows). Paying 7 s on every cold create to
  // save ~28 s on every prebuilt one is the trade, and it is stated here rather than assumed.
  //
  // The three prebuild SETTINGS the contract names — trigger "on configuration change", region
  // EuropeWest, template history 1 — are NOT expressible in this file. They are server-side
  // operator UI state with no public API (probed 2026-08-21: REST 404, GraphQL introspection
  // empty, no `gh codespace` subcommand). They are declared in `.devcontainer/provisioning.yaml`
  // and `scripts/cloud_provisioning.py prebuild` reports declaration-vs-live drift.
  "onCreateCommand": "bash .devcontainer/provision.sh",

  // Provision once per container creation: install pinned uv, unshallow, sync, arm hooks,
  // assert all four legs, run the gate-liveness smoke, then stamp. Kept alongside
  // `onCreateCommand` above rather than replaced by it: a prebuilt image is built from the
  // DEFAULT branch, so a codespace created later — or from a different branch — can carry pins
  // the prebuild never saw. This is the run that re-asserts against the tree actually checked out.
  "postCreateCommand": "bash .devcontainer/provision.sh",

  // Re-assert on EVERY start. A container resumed from a stale image, or one whose repo has
  // since moved its uv pin, is half-provisioned — and this refuses it instead of handing a
  // lane an environment whose gates do not execute.
  "postStartCommand": "bash .devcontainer/provision.sh --gate",

  // Do not report the container ready — or attach a session to it — until provisioning has
  // actually finished. Without this the window between "container up" and "hooks armed" is
  // exactly the arming race this row exists to close.
  "waitFor": "postStartCommand",

  // Codespaces free tier is a 2-core / 8 GB machine; declaring the floor keeps a lane from
  // being scheduled somewhere that cannot run the suite. Storage is deliberately NOT declared:
  // raising it forces a larger (billable) machine type, and stage 1 is a €0 build.
  "hostRequirements": {
    "cpus": 2,
    "memory": "8gb"
  },

  "remoteUser": "vscode",

  "customizations": {
    "vscode": {
      "extensions": [
        "ms-python.python",
        "charliermarsh.ruff"
      ]
    }
  }
}
